; Mem - raw memory operations, in Axiom.
;
; These are the functions that used to require FFI bindings to
; `malloc`/`memcpy`/`memset`. They are now ordinary Axiom loops over the
; `__alloc`/`__load8`/`__store8` primitives, which means they are
; type-checked, span-checked, and steppable like any other Axiom code,
; and a compiled program needs no libc.
;
; Addresses are plain `Int`s. That is deliberately unsafe at this layer -
; it is the layer where Axiom's type system stops and the machine
; begins - and every safe abstraction above it (`Str`, `Buf`) is
; responsible for never handing out an address a caller could misuse.
; Allocate `bytes` bytes of zeroed memory and return its address.
;
; Memory comes from the arena the compiler emits (a bump allocator over
; `mmap`), so allocation is a pointer bump in the common case and there
; is no `free`: an Axiom process reclaims everything at exit. That is
; the right trade for a compiler, and the wrong one for a long-running
; server - which is why this is `memAlloc` and not `new`. (This comment
; used to add "the allocator is replaceable: define `axiom_alloc` and
; the backend defers to it". It does not defer - the backend emits its
; definition unconditionally, and a program defining `axiom_alloc` is
; refused outright, AX3026; it used to pass `check` and die in `opt`
; as a duplicate symbol. docs/memory-model.md MM-ALLOC-8; the explicit
; arena primitives ARE the reclamation, MM-ALLOC-22 - reference
; counting, MM-LIFE-2a, was withdrawn, and this line used to promise it.)
;
; ZEROED IS A PROMISE, not an artefact of where the memory came from,
; and this comment used to be describing the artefact. Fresh pages
; arrive zeroed from the kernel, so the promise held for free until
; `__axiom_arena_reset` began handing the same bytes out a second
; time - after which `strAlloc` produced strings with no terminator
; (`cstrLen` of a 3-byte string measured 17) and a `Map`'s state array
; came back full of a previous table's occupancy. The allocator now
; tracks how far into each chunk it has ever handed out, and scrubs
; below that line as it allocates. Callers may rely on the promise;
; `Map`, `Intern`, `Str` and `Vec` all do.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: memAlloc (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (memAlloc bytes) (__alloc bytes))

; The same allocation, declaring which of the block's words hold
; REFERENCES: bit i of `map` says payload word i is a handle to
; another counted block, so releasing this block releases that one
; too (docs/memory-model.md MM-LIFE-2d, the record form).
;
; `memAlloc` cannot answer this and never will - it sees a byte count
; and nothing else, so it stamps every block a LEAF, which is the
; unsafe layer staying the unsafe layer. A module that builds a header
; with a handle in it - `Str`'s owner word is the first - has to say
; so, once, at the allocation.
;
; The map is CLAMPED here to the block just allocated and to the
; 47-word capacity of the encoding, so this can name the wrong word of
; your own block and cannot name a word outside it. Getting the bit
; wrong is the same class of mistake as getting a `memSetWord` index
; wrong, with one difference worth stating plainly: a word wrongly
; marked as a reference is released at this block's death, and a
; release of something that is not a counted block is a wild write,
; not a wrong answer. Mark only words you allocated and own.
;
; NO NEW PRIMITIVE. The shape word is an ordinary word at `h - 8` and
; the encoding is arithmetic, so this is six operations over
; `__load64`/`__store64` rather than a fifth entry in the backend's
; primitive table - which matters because `stdlib/` is compiled by the
; SEED, and a standard library that spells a primitive the committed
; seed does not know cannot be built at all until the seed moves. The
; clamp is the same clamp either way; what changes is who has to be
; reseeded to get it.
;
; A zero-byte request keeps MM-ALLOC-8b's behaviour exactly: no block,
; no header, nothing stamped.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: memAllocMapped (-> Int Int Int))

(pub fn (memAllocMapped bytes map)
  (let ((h (__alloc bytes)))
    (if (== bytes 0)
      h
      (let ((sw (__load64 (- h 8) 0)))
        (let ((cnt (& (>> sw 1) 16383)))
          (let ((lim 
            (if (> cnt 47)
              47
              cnt
            )
          ))
            {
              (__store64 (- h 8) 0 (| sw (<< (& map (- (<< 1 lim) 1)) 16)))
              h
            }
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )
)

; The ARRAY FORM: payload words 0..n-1 of this block are handles to
; other counted blocks, so releasing it releases all of them, and `n`
; is the caller's ELEMENT count (docs/memory-model.md MM-LIFE-2d names
; the two forms; the array form landed 2026-08-24 and took its own
; length 2026-09-03).
;
; It is one bit - bit 15 of the shape word - and no second header word.
; The LENGTH goes in bits 16..62, which the record form uses for its
; reference bitmap and this form therefore has to itself: the two forms
; are disjoint by construction, so the field can be a bitmap for one
; and a count for the other.
;
; THE LENGTH IS THE CALLER'S, AND IT HAS TO BE. Until 2026-09-03 this
; function took only the handle and the walk read the length out of the
; ALLOCATOR's word count in bits 1..14 - a field the allocator clamps
; to 0 past 16,383 words, because it is a size class and a size class
; it cannot represent is one it refuses to pool. A container's element
; buffer of 131,072 bytes or more therefore announced itself as an
; array of ZERO handles, and `vecFree` released the block and none of
; the elements. Measured: a 16,384-element `vecNewRef` and the same
; program on `vecNew` had the same peak RSS to the kilobyte.
;
; No spelling of this function could recover a number the allocator had
; already thrown away, so the count comes in as an argument - every
; caller has it, because every caller just asked for a buffer of that
; many elements. `tests/stdlib/406-array-form-large-block.ax` is the
; fixture; it answers 22 against the one-argument version and 31
; against this one.
;
; PASS THE ELEMENT COUNT, NOT THE BLOCK'S WORDS. `memAlloc` rounds up
; to 16 bytes, so a block of an odd number of words carries a pad word
; the caller never names; walking 0..n-1 covers exactly the elements
; and steps over the pad, which is strictly safer than the padded count
; the walk used to read.
;
; WHY A CONTAINER CANNOT USE `memAllocMapped` INSTEAD. The record form's
; bitmap holds 47 words. `Intern`'s string vector is **64 words at
; construction**, before anything is interned, so the standard library
; builds a structure past that capacity by default - and a `Vec` that
; grows twice is past it four times over. A count is the only encoding
; that describes a buffer.
;
; THE ELEMENTS PAST `len` MUST BE ZERO. The walk releases words
; 0..count-1 of the whole BLOCK, not 0..len-1: the block knows its size
; and nothing else. `memAlloc` zeroes, which covers a fresh block and a
; recycled one, and a container that vacates a slot - `vecPop`,
; `mapRemove` - has to zero it, or the walk spends a share of a handle
; the caller still holds. That is a use-after-free rather than a leak,
; and it is the one obligation this form puts on its user.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: memMarkArray (-> Int Int Int))

; `n` is masked to 47 bits before it is shifted, so a count nobody can
; hold cannot push a 1 into bit 63 and make the shape word negative -
; `codegen.ax`'s `shapeBits` rests on that word being non-negative.
(pub fn (memMarkArray h n)
  {
    (__store64 (- h 8) 0 (| (| (__load64 (- h 8) 0) 32768) (<< (& n 140737488355327) 16)))
    h
  }
)

; The inverse, and it is not symmetry for its own sake: it is what a
; container's GROWTH needs. Doubling a buffer copies the elements to a
; new block WITHOUT retaining them - the shares move - so releasing the
; old block while it still reads as an array would spend every share
; twice. Clearing the bit first makes the old block a leaf, and its
; release then reclaims the block and touches nothing it used to hold.
;
; IT CLEARS THE LENGTH TOO, and that half is load-bearing rather than
; tidy. Bits 16..62 are the record form's REFERENCE BITMAP, so a block
; that dropped bit 15 and kept its length would not read as a leaf at
; all - it would read as a record whose map names whichever payload
; words the length's set bits happen to fall on, and release would
; spend a share of each. A buffer of 8,192 elements would name word 13.
; That is a wild write rather than a leak, and it is why the mask here
; and the count in `memMarkArray` are one change and not two.
;
; WHICH MEANS THIS IS NOT A NO-OP ON A RECORD. The mask used to clear
; bit 15 alone, so calling it on a record-mapped block left the map
; standing; it now clears the map too. Both callers in this tree hand
; it an ARRAY-FORM buffer - `vecReserveExactly`'s abandoned data block
; and `mapRehashCap`'s abandoned value array, neither of which ever
; carried a record map - and the name says what it does: it makes the
; block a leaf, and a leaf has no map.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: memMarkLeaf (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (memMarkLeaf h)
  {
    (__store64 (- h 8) 0 (& (__load64 (- h 8) 0) 32767))
    h
  }
)

; THERE IS NO `memIsArray`, AND THE REASON IS A MEASURED CRASH.
;
; The obvious companion to the two above is a reader - "does this block
; carry the array form" - and the first version of this work had one.
; It is not sound, because bit 15 is only unambiguous against an
; allocator that clamps at 16,383 words, and this file is compiled by
; whichever compiler is building it. The seed that made this a crash
; clamped at 32,767, which is where the bit used to be the top of the
; count field, so under THAT seed's runtime every block of 16,384 words
; or more had bit 15 set and read back as an array of handles.
;
; `tests/stdlib/200-scale.ax` interns 100,000 strings, so its `Map`
; reaches 262,144 slots, so its value array passes 16,384 words - and
; `mapRemove` read the bit, believed it, and released a raw integer.
; SIGSEGV, in a program that is correct under the compiler this tree
; builds and wrong under the one that builds this tree. `stage1` runs
; on the seed's runtime, so the bootstrap ladder is exactly where this
; lands.
;
; THAT PARTICULAR SEED IS GONE, AND THE RULE IS NOT. Checked
; 2026-09-03: the committed seeds (09f3eb4) all carry
; `icmp ugt i64 %wcnt, 16383` and `%aform = and i64 %shw, 32768`, so
; today's seed agrees with today's compiler and a reader would not
; crash on this tree. It would crash on the NEXT tree whose allocator
; moves, which is the same hazard with a different date on it - a
; reader here is sound only by accident of what happens to be in
; `bootstrap/`, and this file cannot see what that is. The flag word
; stays.
;
; A container therefore carries its own flag in its own header - one
; word it wrote itself, with no encoding to be ambiguous about - and
; the shape bit stays what it is for: the RUNTIME's instruction for
; walking a dead block, written by the same compiler that emits the
; walk that reads it.
; Copy `count` bytes from `src` to `dst`. Returns `dst`.
;
; Copies forwards, so overlapping ranges are only safe when
; `dst <= src`; `memMove` is not provided yet rather than being
; provided wrongly.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: memCopy (-> Int Int Int Int))

(pub fn (memCopy dst src count) (memCopyFrom dst src count 0))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHY THESE THREE ARE LOOPS AND NOT RECURSIONS
;
; Each walks one byte per step, so its depth is the SIZE OF THE DATA -
; a 100 KB copy is 100,000 frames. When this block was written the
; recursive form was a real call chain ("stage1 emits no tail-call
; optimisation at all") that overflowed the stack once the compiler's
; own output grew past a few hundred kilobytes, surfacing as a SIGSEGV
; inside `memCopyFrom` that looked like a codegen bug in whatever was
; being compiled. That REASON is stale: the compiler now turns a direct
; self tail call into a loop at every --opt level, in its own codegen
; (docs/memory-model.md MM-EXEC-6b).
;
; The SPELLING stays, for the rule that outlives the reason: the
; standard library must not owe its stack safety to an optimisation or
; to a call's exact shape. A mutual or let-bound tail call is still a
; real call chain without `opt` (MM-EXEC-6c), and one refactor away is
; all it takes to turn the former into the latter. A `while` loop is
; flat by construction (`tests/selfhost/500-while-mut.ax`).
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(:: memCopyFrom (-> Int Int Int Int Int))

(fn (memCopyFrom dst src count start)
  (let ((mut i start))
    {
      (while (< i count)
        {
          (__store8 dst i (__load8 src i))
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      dst
    }
  )
)

; Set `count` bytes at `addr` to `value` (low 8 bits). Returns `addr`.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: memSet (-> Int Int Int Int))

(pub fn (memSet addr value count) (memSetFrom addr value count 0))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(:: memSetFrom (-> Int Int Int Int Int))

(fn (memSetFrom addr value count start)
  (let ((mut i start))
    {
      (while (< i count)
        {
          (__store8 addr i value)
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      addr
    }
  )
)

; Compare `count` bytes. 0 if equal, otherwise the signed difference of
; the first differing byte pair (so the result orders like `memcmp`).
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: memCmp (-> Int Int Int Int))

(pub fn (memCmp a b count) (memCmpFrom a b count 0))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(:: memCmpFrom (-> Int Int Int Int Int))

(fn (memCmpFrom a b count start)
  (let (
    (mut i start)
    (mut diff 0)
  )
    {
      (while (&& (< i count) (== diff 0))
        {
          (set diff (- (__load8 a i) (__load8 b i)))
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      diff
    }
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Word-granular access
;
; Offsets here are in *words* (8 bytes), not bytes, because the things
; built on them - the `Str` header, `Buf`, and every `data` value the
; compiler boxes - are arrays of machine words.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The word at `index`. A word is what it is: an integer, or a handle,
; or a reference whose type this layer does not know. It answers `Int`
; because that is the truth about a machine word - it used to answer a
; type variable, which let the CALLER name any type at all and get it,
; including a reference, which then dereferenced. See `AX3040`.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: memGetWord (-> Int Int Int))

(pub fn (memGetWord addr index) (__load64 addr index))

; The String view, for the typed accessors built on this layer -
; `tokenLexeme`, `diagCode`, and the several dozen others whose own
; signature says `String` and whose body is one word read.
;
; The cast is HERE, at a return inside a signature that carries the
; type, and deliberately not at those call sites: a cast at an argument
; root classifies the value's evidence 0 and drops its retain or its
; release (docs/memory-model.md MM-LIFE-2e, measured).
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: memGetWordStr (-> Int Int String))

(pub fn (memGetWordStr addr index)
  (cast String (memGetWord addr index))
)

; The `Vec` view of the word at `index`. A `Vec` is a handle - one
; word, exactly what `memGetWord` answers - so this reinterprets and
; converts nothing. The cast is HERE, at a return inside a signature
; that carries the type, for the reason `memGetWordStr` gives: a cast
; at an argument root classifies that value's evidence 0 and drops its
; retain or its release (docs/memory-model.md MM-VAL-22, measured).
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: memGetWordVec (-> Int Int (Vec a)))

(pub fn (memGetWordVec addr index)
  (cast (Vec a) (memGetWord addr index))
)

; Storing a word here is the moment a value can leave the type
; system's sight: `(cast Int value)` erases whatever `value` was, and
; the machine word that lands in `addr` is indistinguishable from an
; integer forever after. That is the whole of MM-LIFE-2c's
; co-ownership blocker, and the fix is one line - the store takes a
; SHARE of what it is about to hide.
;
; `__retainref` is type-directed, so this is free where it should be:
; the overwhelming majority of word stores put an integer somewhere,
; the call's evidence witness is 0, and NOTHING is emitted. A
; reference costs one retain, and a type variable - `vecPush`'s
; element, `mapInsert`'s value - costs a test of one bit of the
; caller's evidence word.
;
; The share is deliberately NOT balanced by a release, and cannot be:
; nothing tells this layer when the word is overwritten or the block
; dies, which is exactly why the layer is unsafe. So a reference
; stored through here is immortal, and that is a LEAK - the safe
; direction, and no worse than before, since a value only reachable
; from `memAlloc`'d memory was never reclaimed anyway. What it buys
; is that the value is no longer INVISIBLE: an owner elsewhere may
; now hand back its own share without freeing a block this word still
; points at, which is the precondition every remaining ownership
; event was waiting on.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: memSetWord (-> Int Int a Int))

(pub fn (memSetWord addr index value)
  {
    (__retainref value)
    (__store64
      addr      index      (cast Int value)
    )
    addr
  }
)

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: memGetByte (-> Int Int Int))

(pub fn (memGetByte addr index) (__load8 addr index))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: memPutByte (-> Int Int Int Int))

(pub fn (memPutByte addr index value)
  {
    (__store8 addr index value)
    addr
  }
)
